+- **The universal rule** is `a11y-overrides.css`. Under `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`,
+  opacity and colour transitions keep a clock of 150 ms or less; transform, translate,
+  scale, size and filter transitions and every non-ambient animation stop at their end
+  state, while the element's opacity fade still runs; ambient loops stop. The rule carries
+  no `!important` that a consumer cannot override from unlayered CSS, so a site's own fade
+  keeps its clock. (O-350, the orchestrator's ruling on this precept, placed for 11.1.1
+  and flagged to the owner as his to reverse.) `[data-allow-motion]` is not carved here:
+  a user who asks for reduced motion outranks a per-element allowance (accessibility is
+  absolute). The attribute does one other job: it keeps an element's own transition
+  through the theme flip's `html.no-transition`. The `DarkModeToggle`'s glyph does not
+  move under reduced motion: its change rides the theme's one crossfade, an opacity
+  change on the compositor of 150 ms (O-428; O-353's ruling on the theme flip).
+- **What 11.1.1 ships falls short of it.** 11.1.1 built the rule's last clause and not its
+  first: its rule, in its own last layer, runs every transition on a zero clock ("the
+  instant fallback", `a11y-overrides.css:6`), and `view-transition.css` sets
+  `animation: none !important` on every view-transition pseudo, so every fade no part
+  carves is a cut. Both are shortfalls of the rule above, not its mechanism. The BM
+  tranche's springs wave (BM-3) builds the rule in its parts minor unless BL's 11.1.2
